Microtasks (Promise callbacks, MutationObserver) run immediately after the current stack and before the next macrotask. Macrotasks (setTimeout, setImmediate, I/O) are queued after microtasks. In the snippet below, the promise resolves before the setTimeout callback, so its .then runs first:

```js
Promise.resolve().then(()=>console.log('micro'));
setTimeout(()=>console.log('macro'),0);
console.log('sync');
```
Output: sync, micro, macro. If you nest a promise inside setTimeout, the promise’s microtasks still run after the timeout callback but before the next macrotask, which can surprise developers expecting the promise to run before the timeout. Understanding this ordering is critical for deterministic async flows.

Use a helper that returns a promise with retry logic and cancellation token. Example:

```js
async function fetchWithRetry(url, opts, retries=3){
  for(let i=0;i<retries;i++){
    try{ return await fetch(url,opts); }
    catch(e){ if(i===retries-1) throw e; }
  }
}

async function loadAll(){
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const [a,b] = await Promise.all([
    fetchWithRetry('/api/a',{signal:controller.signal}),
    fetchWithRetry('/api/b',{signal:controller.signal})
  ]).catch(err=>{ /* handle partial */ });
  // combine a,b
}
```

Trade‑offs: Promise.all fails fast; use Promise.allSettled for partial success. Cancellation via AbortController stops network requests but not already started promises. Retries increase latency but improve resilience.
